313 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/T (The transition from GOW50 to GOW30 does not stand alone. It forms part of a broader tradition of mobility reforms in Amsterdam, prioritising safe, accessible, and human-centred roads. Within this wider policy agenda, the challenge is not only technical, i.e. how to manage traffic flow efficiently at lower speeds, but organisational and cultural: how to redesign arterial roads so they look and feel like 30 km/h environments, and how to support behavioural adaptation from residents and road users.)
/E (The transition from GOW50 to GOW30 does not stand alone. It forms part of a broader tradition of mobility reforms in Amsterdam, prioritising safe, accessible, and human-centred roads. Within this wider policy agenda, the challenge is not only technical, i.e. how to manage traffic flow efficiently at lower speeds, but organisational and cultural: how to redesign arterial roads so they look and feel like 30 km/h environments, and how to support behavioural adaptation from residents and road users.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
314 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/T (Recent evaluation of the policy in a published report by the Municipality of Amsterdam \(2025\) further underlines the relevance of this topic. The analysis shows significant improvements in perceived and measured road safety, reductions in noise levels, and generally positive liveability outcomes. Importantly, concerns regarding reduced accessibility or worsening congestion have not materialised to the degree initially feared. With the first effects now strongly in place, the question shifts from why Amsterdam implemented GOW30 to how these roads should be redesigned to true low-speed environments, and how the decision-making process behind such redesigns can be improved.)
/E (Recent evaluation of the policy in a published report by the Municipality of Amsterdam \(2025\) further underlines the relevance of this topic. The analysis shows significant improvements in perceived and measured road safety, reductions in noise levels, and generally positive liveability outcomes. Importantly, concerns regarding reduced accessibility or worsening congestion have not materialised to the degree initially feared. With the first effects now strongly in place, the question shifts from why Amsterdam implemented GOW30 to how these roads should be redesigned to true low-speed environments, and how the decision-making process behind such redesigns can be improved.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
