318 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Figure 1: The 2023 GOW30 policy change, mapping where all roads in blue were lowered in speed from 50 km/h to 30 km/h \(Municipality of Amsterdam, 2025\).)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
319 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/T <>
/E <>
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
320 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (2)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
321 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (To begin with, a key challenge emerging from the transition relates to its public acceptance and perceived legitimacy. Even though the policy has produced measurable improvements, questions persist about whether the measure restricts motor-vehicle accessibility or signals an unintended reduction of car use in the city. These concerns highlight a need for ways that can clearly communicate how the wider social and environmental benefits of GOW30 outweigh marginal losses in travel speed and efficiency \(Municipality of Amsterdam, 2025\).)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
322 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (A second challenge arises from a misalignment between road design and the intended speed behaviour. Many roads designated now as GOW30 still resemble their former 50 km/h layout, lacking visual design cues that intuitively support slower driving. This discrepancy negatively affects the credibility of the speed limit, as the physical environment communicates a higher speed than the legally posted limit implies. Consequently, the road fails to intuitively guide driver behaviour, creating a disconnect between the rule and the reality \(Gargoum et al., 2016\). This mismatch highlights the importance of examining where and how design interventions are required to create credible and self-explaining 30 km/h roads.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
323 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Another challenge concerns the complex decision-making environment around GOW30 redesign. The process involves a broad set of stakeholders: e.g. municipal departments, consultants, public transport companies, and civic groups, all of which have different interests. Without a structured guidelines for evaluating design options or weighing competing interests, design choices risk becoming opaque, inconsistent, or overly reliant on professional intuition. Addressing this requires a more transparent and collaborative approach in which decisions can be systematically explored and compared by all stakeholders involved.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
324 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (1.3 Research scope, aim, and questions )
/E (1.3 Research scope, aim, and questions )
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [14]
>>
endobj
325 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Current decision-making processes affecting the design of and resulting speed limit compliance on GOW30 roads remain under-researched.  The aim of this research is to explore the effect of utilising a public dialogue approach and decision-making support tool to make the decision-making process for road redesigns of GOW30 roads in Amsterdam more transparent and collaborative between key stakeholders. The value of public dialogue lies in the practical application of the Living Lab method.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [1 2]
>>
endobj
326 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Following this, the central research question to this project is: )
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [3 4]
>>
endobj
327 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (How can a decision-making support tool contribute to the public dialogue on the governance and implementation of GOW30 in Amsterdam?)
/Pg 12 0 R
>>
endobj
