342 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (This chapter outlines the research process used to explore GOW30 \(re\)design and decision-making in Amsterdam. It explains the theoretical grounding, the methods applied, and the sequential phases of our Living Lab approach, showing how theory, expert insights, and policy analysis collectively shaped our understanding of the relevant challenges and opportunities.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
343 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (2.1 Literature review)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
344 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (This literature review covers three key theoretical fields: driver behaviour and road design, explaining how environment and compliance interrelate; the GOW30 policy framework in Amsterdam, outlining current governance and implementation practices; and decision-making theory and support tools, providing the foundation for developing a structured tool that enhances dialogue and decision-making on GOW30 design.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
345 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/T (2.1.1 Designing roads to steer compliance )
/E (2.1.1 Designing roads to steer compliance )
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
346 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Previous research on driver speed compliance demonstrates that traffic behaviour arises from a continuous interaction between cognitive processes, personal characteristics, and the surrounding road environment. Yadav & Velaga \(2021\) show that compliance is strongly context-dependent, with drivers adapting more effectively to lower speeds in urban settings than in rural ones. Their findings highlight that the perceived credibility of a speed limit depends on how intuitively the environment communicates it, pointing to the psychological and cognitive foundations of speed choice. Gargoum et al. \(2016\) extend this cognitive-environmental relationship by examining how specific physical road features influence compliance. While Yadav & Velaga demonstrate that environment broadly affects how drivers interpret and adapt to speed limits, Gargoum et al. quantify the influence of concrete design elements such as lane widths, roadside friction, and visual enclosure. Their work shows that compliance increases when the physical form of the road aligns with the intended limit. This positions road characteristics not merely as a contextual backdrop but as a primary behavioural determinant that can actively steer drivers toward compliance.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
347 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Orsi et al. \(2025\) build on these behavioural and environmental insights by introducing a computational method that operationalises them. Using machine-learning models trained on road-view imagery, they quantify how visible design characteristics affect expected compliance. Their research synthesizes the previous concepts into a predictive framework capable of estimating driver compliance from the visual attributes of a road. Together, these studies present a clear conceptual baseline: cognitive tendencies shape behaviour, environmental cues modulate those tendencies, and emerging computational tools can now measure and predict this relationship at larger scales.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
348 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Efforts to improve speed limit compliance in urban environments tend to follow two complementary design theories: self-explaining roads and traffic calming measures. Both aim to reduce driver speed, yet they operate through different yet intertwining behavioural mechanisms.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
349 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E <>
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
350 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (5)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
351 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (When self-explaining conditions cannot be fully achieved, which happens particularly on arterial roads with mixed functions, traffic calming measures \(TCMs\) become necessary. TCMs encompass physical or visual interventions explicitly intended to reduce vehicle speeds, such as speed bumps, chicanes, narrowed lanes, raised crossings, or gateways. Distefano & Leonardi \(2022\) show that TCMs create a fluctuating speed profile that forces drivers to slow down at strategic points, compensating for road environments that do not inherently induce compliant behaviour. Pirdavani et al. \(2025\) further highlight the usefulness of TCMs in high-to-low speed transition zones, suggesting that well-designed gateway elements can reduce reliance on costly enforcement and support the broader goal of creating self-explanatory networks.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
352 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (TERs and TCMs are inherently connected and share a lot of overlap in execution, however they are key in understanding the mechanisms behind speed limit compliance. Together they form the design foundation for understanding how GOW30 roads can become both legible and credible to drivers.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
353 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/T (2.1.2 GOW30 road-category )
/E (2.1.2 GOW30 road-category )
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
354 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E <>
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [7 8]
>>
endobj
355 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Because these roads served both as places where people live, walk, and cycle, and as corridors for motor-vehicle movement, they no longer fitted neatly into either category.)
/Pg 15 0 R
>>
endobj
356 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (This mismatch created situations where the functional role of the road conflicted with its design and user composition, producing safety risks, higher speeds, and lower liveability. The introduction of the GOW30 category was therefore intended to remove this ambiguity by offering a design and regulatory framework that could better balance road capacity and the societal goals of safety and liveability within built-up urban contexts \(CROW, 2021b; Municipality of Amsterdam, 2024; SWOV, 2019\). )
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [1 2]
>>
endobj
357 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E <>
/Pg 15 0 R
>>
endobj
358 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (The report by Municipality of Amsterdam \(2025\) localises the initial outcomes of the GOW30 principles by documenting how the city-wide transition has thus far improved safety and liveability. The report notes reductions in crash rates, noise levels, and perceived traffic pressure, highlighting several roads where lower speeds have directly enabled more space for pedestrians and cyclists. These effects intersect with a broader cultural shift in Amsterdam, where the 30 km/h limit is becoming the normative urban standard and where car use is increasingly deprioritised. Compared with other Dutch municipalities, Amsterdam adopts a more assertive interpretation of the national mandate, raising questions about how local political ambition and spatial conditions shape differentiated municipal strategies toward GOW30 implementation.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
359 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (6)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
360 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/T (2.1.3 Design process: decision-making tools & public dialogue )
/E (2.1.3 Design process: decision-making tools & public dialogue )
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
361 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Aside from the technical considerations that need to be studied when re-designing a road, one may also look at the way in which these decisions on interventions are made. There are multiple theoretical understandings which guide this process. Decision-making in traffic management has traditionally been dominated by top-down, expert-driven planning approaches. However, Immers & van der Knaap \(2011\) argue for a shift toward a living systems approach, in which traffic systems are viewed as dynamic and adaptive rather than static or centrally controlled. Within such a system, each stakeholder, whether it is a policymaker, designer, or user, plays an integral role in maintaining balance and resilience. This argument fits within the broader governance principles outlined by Tasan-Kok \(2021\), who describes urban governance as inherently multi-actor, involving the interplay of institutional, professional and civic stakeholders whose interests and power relations must be managed rather than simplified. When applied to GOW30, this theoretical framing suggests that road redesigns should not solely reflect technical criteria but also respond to social and institutional dynamics across various scales. )
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [1 2]
>>
endobj
362 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E <>
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
363 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Finally, Aspen \(2023\) demonstrates in particular how decision-making \(support\) tools can operationalize this integration by translating data-led insights into participatory scenarios. Rather than replacing human reasoning, these tools facilitate transparency and collective learning among stakeholders. By modelling different spatial or behavioural outcomes, they support more informed and balanced governance decisions. Within the Amsterdam GOW30 transition, such approaches may help structure stakeholder interactions, clarify design priorities, and create a shared language between engineers, policymakers, and urban designers. Ultimately strengthening both the governance and implementation of low-speed road designs.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
364 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/T (2.1.4 Conceptual framework )
/E (2.1.4 Conceptual framework )
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
365 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (The conceptual diagram synthesizes the theoretical foundations discussed by tracing how insights from cognitive driver behaviour, road design theory, and decision-making approaches collectively shape the implementation of GOW30 in Amsterdam. The starting point here is the recognition that driver behaviour is context-dependent: both cognitive tendencies and environmental cues determine whether drivers comply with a lower speed limit, as demonstrated in behavioural studies. This behavioural core directly feeds into two major strands of design theory: self-explaining roads and traffic-calming measures. Both strategies translate behavioural insights into design principles and together form the theoretical backbone of the GOW30 design framework shown in the diagram.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
