>>
endobj
370 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (The GOW30 design framework represents the methodological centre of the project: it formalises how behavioural knowledge and environmental factors can be translated into \(categorised\) criteria for evaluating and selecting appropriate GOW30 interventions. This aligns with the broader theory on decision-making tools in transportation planning, which emphasises structure and transparency in complex urban design processes. However, theory also highlights that improved decision-making does not rely on traditional \(design\) planning alone. Implementation requires navigating multiple institutional actors, perspectives and priorities. )
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [4 5]
>>
endobj
371 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (The real knowledge gap here lies in the practicalities of achieving this effectively through active stakeholder engagement. For that reason, the diagram incorporates co-creative decision-making as an essential methodological complement to the design framework. Co-creation connects technical reasoning with experiential and professional knowledge from different stakeholders, helping to bridge gaps between behavioural theory, institutional constraints, and practical implementation. The precise interaction between co-creative decision-making and the GOW30 design framework is the key analytical relation to this research \(as represented by the question mark indicating this is also the knowledge gap\), In the end, this interrelation converges towards the same outcome: improved implementation of GOW30. The diagram thus illustrates how theoretical insights on behaviour, design strategies, and decision-making processes interact to support a more credible, effective, and context-sensitive transition to 30 km/h roads in Amsterdam.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
