429 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (The key method utilised in this research was hosting a co-creation session aligned to the Living Lab handbook by Dijkstra & Joore \(2025\). Co-creation is the practice of collaborating with diverse set of stakeholders to facilitate meaningful participation strengthening the design process. Collaborative design efforts can help us understand the stakeholders' needs better and lead to unexpected ideas. )
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [11 12]
>>
endobj
430 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (For this, we invited a focused group of stakeholders directly involved in the technical and governance aspects of GOW30 road design. This included our project partners from the DRO-DMI consortium \(Municipality of Amsterdam, AMS Institute, and Goudappel\), consultancy firm DTV, knowledge institutes SWOV and CROW, and advocacy group Fietsersbond. We deliberately limited the group to professional organizations to allow for deeper discussions on design logic, technical challenges, and decision-making structures. The co-creation session consisted of two main parts. )
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [6 7]
>>
endobj
431 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Design workshop)
/Pg 22 0 R
>>
endobj
432 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (The first part of the session focused on collecting input from the stakeholders by explaining our logic behind the tool to the stakeholders as well as demonstrating the prototype on an example case of Pieter Calandlaan in Amsterdam. We used an interactive workshop format to actively engage the stakeholders. After a brief presentation from our side introducing the Living Lab method and demonstrating our tool, stakeholders were divided into small mixed teams of two to three people. Each team consisted of experts with diverse backgrounds to encourage diverse perspectives.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
433 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (For the workshop, the teams worked through a structured worksheet \(Appendix 2\) consisting of 3 parts. First, the stakeholders listed and elaborated on criteria they consider most important when redesigning a road. Second, each team selected their four most important criteria and translated them into concrete design measures that could be applied to a GOW30 road. In the final part, teams applied these measures to the example case by drawing a simple cross-section of the Pieter Calandlaan, showcasing how their measures could change the road. To wrap up the workshop, each team briefly presented their design to the group.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
