/E <>
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
437 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T (Due to the dual goals of this part of the session, all tension points were grouped into two overarching themes. Each theme consists of six statements \(twelve in total; see Appendix 3\). The first theme, Governance, Design and Institutional Dynamics, encompasses dilemmas such as top-down regulation versus co-created input, central standards versus local adaptation, and siloed departments versus integrated ownership. The second theme, Behavioural Change, Design and Social Norms, reflects expert insights on enforcement, self-explaining roads and cultural adaptation, expressed through tensions like enforcement versus design, individual compliance versus collective responsibility, and rapid policy roll-out versus gradual norm change.)
/E (Due to the dual goals of this part of the session, all tension points were grouped into two overarching themes. Each theme consists of six statements \(twelve in total; see Appendix 3\). The first theme, Governance, Design and Institutional Dynamics, encompasses dilemmas such as top-down regulation versus co-created input, central standards versus local adaptation, and siloed departments versus integrated ownership. The second theme, Behavioural Change, Design and Social Norms, reflects expert insights on enforcement, self-explaining roads and cultural adaptation, expressed through tensions like enforcement versus design, individual compliance versus collective responsibility, and rapid policy roll-out versus gradual norm change.)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [2]
