/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (In the Netherlands, cycling infrastructure can generally be found in a number of distinct types. Firstly, on-road bicycle lanes \(fietsstroken\) are marked lanes on the carriageway itself, typically distinguished by red asphalt, white edge lines, and a painted bicycle symbol, signalling to traffic users that this space is primarily intended for cyclists \(see Figure 7a\). Secondly, fully separated bicycle lanes \(fietspaden\) are physically segregated from the main roadway, providing cyclists with their own dedicated space separated from motorised traffic \(see Figure 7b\). )
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [14 15]
>>
endobj
449 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (CROW distinguishes these facilities primarily by the level of traffic intensity and the function of the road \(CROW, 2021a, 2023\). For standard arterial roads with a maximum speed of 50km/h, referred to as GOW50, it prescribes a baseline bicycle solution of fully separated bicycle lanes, or redirecting cyclists to parallel service roads \(ventwegen\), which commonly have a lower maximum speed and therefore more appropriate in safely accommodating cyclists. Due to the large difference in speed between motorists and cyclists, mixing these two kinds of traffic users is discouraged to ensure safety \(CROW, 2023\).)
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [4 5]
>>
endobj
450 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E <>
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [6 7]
>>
endobj
