>>
endobj
452 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Figure 7: \(a.\) represents a road layout with fietsstroken, while \(b.\) represents a road with fietspaden \(Goudappel, 2025\).)
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [16]
>>
endobj
453 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Since GOW30 combines both the traffic flow function of a GOW50 and a staying function of a ETW30, CROW guidelines for bicycle facilities in a GOW30 recommend a solution that caters to both demands. Unlike ETW roads, CROW prescribes on-road bicycle lanes \(fietsstroken\) for GOW30 as the latter tends to see larger motor traffic volumes. In certain cases however, fully separated bicycle lanes \(fietspaden\) are also deemed appropriate. In its publication regarding GOW30 guidelines, CROW advises to implement fietsstroken on GOW30 roads and fietspaden on GOW50 roads, for the sake of recognisability. )
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [17 18]
>>
endobj
454 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (15)
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
455 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Additionally, the Goudappel \(2025\) report indicates that the determining factor in which facility is optimal lies in the intensity of motorized traffic: if it exceeds 10.000 movements per day a fietspad is recommended. Additionally, the report suggests that available space acts as a constraint; a total road width of approximately 11,8 metres is required in order to apply a fietspad.)
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [0 1 2]
>>
endobj
