/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Discussions emphasised that differentiation becomes particularly relevant where road function shifts. For instance, segments carrying high bus frequencies or acting as local distribution corridors cannot be redesigned in the same way as roads that do not carry these functions. Several panel members questioned whether long corridors should remain uniformly 30 km/h when contextual changes might justify a return to 50 km/h speed limits. )
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [5 6]
>>
endobj
522 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Others warned, however, that allowing exceptions risks weakening the clarity of the newly established uniformity. Even subtle inconsistencies in layout or signage could lead to confusion among drivers but also cyclists and pedestrians, who experience these environments more continuously and are more vulnerable when speeds fluctuate unexpectedly \(Municipality of Amsterdam, 2025\).)
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
523 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (In general, the discussion showed that the largest challenge is not the existence of variation itself, but the absence of a shared framework for when and how differentiation is justified. Without such a structure, decisions risk becoming subjective, difficult to compare across projects, and misaligned between municipal departments.)
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
