537 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (At the start of this project, the working hypothesis was that existing GOW30 guidelines could be translated into clear categories that would directly structure design decisions. The combined results from the policy review, expert interviews, and co-creation activities demonstrated that this assumption was overly linear. Rather than a purely technical challenge, GOW30 design emerged as a socio-technical process shaped by interpretation, negotiation, and institutional context.)
/Pg 32 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
538 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E <>
/Pg 32 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
539 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/T (3.4.1 Living Lab lessons)
/E (3.4.1 Living Lab lessons)
/Pg 32 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
540 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Synthesizing the Living Lab insights highlights several recurring points where theoretical ambitions diverge from everyday practice. Rather than exposing a lack of awareness among stakeholders, these lessons illustrate shared challenges in translating widely recognised issues into actionable decision-making processes. They therefore inform both broader reflections on design governance and the functional logic of the final tool.)
/Pg 32 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
541 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Technical ideals vs. implementation reality)
/Pg 32 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
542 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (The co-creation session revealed a tension between conceptual design ambitions and the practical constraints of implementation. )
/Pg 32 0 R
/K [12 13]
>>
endobj
543 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (While advisory and research-oriented organisations tend to emphasise coherence, recognisability, and long-term design principles, municipal practitioners must account for budget cycles, maintenance planning, and political timing. This does not reflect disagreement over objectives, but rather different operational realities. )
/Pg 32 0 R
/K [3 4]
>>
endobj
544 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Administrative compliance vs. experienced safety)
/Pg 32 0 R
>>
endobj
545 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Across interviews and the panel discussion, participants stressed that formal compliance with design standards does not automatically translate into perceived safety or behavioural change. Roads that technically meet GOW30 requirements may still visually communicate higher speeds, undermining credibility and compliance. This insight reinforces the importance of visual feedback in decision-making. By enabling realistic visualisations of design choices, the tool responds to the need for assessing how roads are experienced in practice rather than relying solely on abstract criteria or numerical thresholds.)
/Pg 32 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
546 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (The narrative of balance vs. reality of silos)
/Pg 32 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
547 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (While policy frameworks promote a balance between traffic efficiency and safety/ liveability, the Living Lab discussions showed that these goals are often addressed through separate institutional lenses. Stakeholders are generally aware of this fragmentation, yet there is limited shared understanding of how to navigate trade-offs collaboratively. Rather than resolving these tensions, decision-making often depends on which perspective dominates a given project. The tool is therefore positioned not as an immediate solution to institutional fragmentation, but rather to make trade-offs explicit and comparable, supporting more transparent dialogue across departments and stakeholder groups.)
/Pg 32 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
