/T (4.1 Conclusion)
/E (4.1 Conclusion)
/Pg 34 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
558 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (This research followed a Living Lab process that iteratively combined academic theory, policy analysis, and expert knowledge through interviews and co-creation to address the challenges surrounding GOW30 design implementation in Amsterdam. Starting from the main problem identified, namely the misalignment between policy ambitions, road design, and behavioural compliance on newly designated 30 km/h arterial roads, the project moved through successive phases of exploration, prototyping, and analysis. Each step informed the next and jointly shaped both the analytical insights and the development of the final digital product.)
/Pg 34 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
559 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E <>
/Pg 34 0 R
/K [5 6]
>>
endobj
560 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E <>
/Pg 34 0 R
/K [7 8]
>>
endobj
561 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Expert insights revealed tensions around the necessity of self-explaining road principles. Whereas municipal practice often relied on signage and gradual adaptation, road safety research consistently argued for physical redesign to achieve credible speed limits. In addition, the technocratic nature of traffic engineering, characterised by specialised expertise and data-heavy reasoning, made design trade-offs difficult to communicate, limiting inclusive and co-creative approaches with non-expert stakeholders.)
/Pg 34 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
562 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E <>
/Pg 34 0 R
/K [10 11]
>>
endobj
563 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Building on this, the framework was translated into an implementation-oriented digital tool that uses AI-modelled visualisations to make the consequences of design choices tangible and accessible. Insights from the Living Lab were iteratively integrated into the tool: all tool aspects were based on policy documents, while the co-creation process validated and refined the contextual criteria and confirmed that its primary use lies in structuring public dialogue. As a result, the tool functions as a shared reference point through which decision-makers can engage not only with consultants and researchers, but also with residents, transport companies, and emergency services.)
/Pg 34 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
564 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (25)
/Pg 34 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
565 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E <>
/Pg 35 0 R
/K [1 2]
>>
endobj
566 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (While this conclusion has clarified the reasoning behind the development of the decision-making support tool and its intended role in GOW30 governance, it does not yet explain how the tool operates in practice. The Living Lab research established why such a tool is needed and what it should enable in terms of dialogue, transparency, and collaboration. The following section therefore shifts from analytical reflection to technical explanation. It showcases how the research insights are translated into practical choices within the product, and explains the underlying structure, logic, and mechanisms of the proof of concept-tool as it currently functions.)
/Pg 35 0 R
/K [3 4]
>>
endobj
